# ─── Models ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A single OpenRouter key covers every model below.
# Model IDs use Mastra's router format: openrouter/<provider>/<model>
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
REPLICATE_API_KEY=

# Cheap, high-volume: scores and labels every incoming headline. Sees text only,
# but must support schema-constrained JSON output — the rank step depends on it.
AVA_MODEL_RSS_RANKER=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# Strong: writes the digest for items that survived ranking. Text in, text out.
AVA_MODEL_RSS_SUMMARIZER=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# Conversational surface in Telegram. Must be vision-capable: images sent to the
# bot are passed inline to the model.
AVA_MODEL_ORCHESTRATOR=openrouter/minimax/minimax-m3
# Weekly memory review: decides which short-term notes are worth keeping for
# good. Schema-constrained JSON output, same as the ranker.
AVA_MODEL_MEMORY_REVIEWER=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# /translearn and /translate: translates an article into your language, glossed
# with the English original. Writes several thousand characters per call, and a
# reader is waiting on it, so latency is felt here in a way it never is on a
# schedule — deepseek-v4-flash ranged from 9s to 283s per part in testing, which
# is why this no longer points at it.
AVA_MODEL_TRANSLATOR=openrouter/openai/gpt-5.6-luna
# /summarize: explains one swiped article at length, in the language you ask for.
# A reader waits on this one too, and it is a single call over the whole article
# rather than one per part — so it wants the same thing the translator does.
AVA_MODEL_SUMMARIZER=openrouter/openai/gpt-5.6-luna
# /follow: turns the subject you typed — in any language — into an English beat
# definition: title, emoji, subtopics, and the bullets its rubric is built from.
# Cheap and schema-constrained like the ranker, and it needs no web search, which
# is the whole reason it is separate from the scout below.
AVA_MODEL_BEAT_NAMER=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# /follow: proposes websites that publish about the subject. One of the two models
# here that need the open web — the job scout below is the other — which is why no
# search dependency was ever added.
# Whatever you put here must actually search: a plain chat model will confidently
# return sites from its training data and the misses are silent.
# sonar over sonar-pro is measured, scoring verified feeds rather than proposed
# sites: 18/24 vs 16/24 across three subjects, faster and cheaper, and sonar-pro
# invented a domain. It never returns feed URLs — code resolves those.
AVA_MODEL_BEAT_SCOUT=openrouter/perplexity/sonar
# /target: turns a sentence about the work you want — in any language — into the
# filters a job search is made of. Cheap and schema-constrained like the beat
# namer, no web search, one call per search ever created. It writes
# configuration, which is a real risk, but a deterministic one: /target echoes
# every field straight back, so a wrong parse is visible and saying it again
# more precisely replaces the row.
AVA_MODEL_JOB_TARGETER=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# The `sonar` job source: asks which companies are hiring for each of your saved
# searches, on the scheduled run. The second model here that needs the open web,
# and the same rule applies — it must actually search, or it will name companies
# that stopped hiring two years ago and the failure will be silent.
# It never returns a careers-page URL: code fetches the company's site, finds the
# page, and requires real text back. Cost is capped by jobScout.maxSearchesPerRun
# in src/config/jobs.ts, not by this variable. Archive the `sonar` row in
# job_sources to switch the whole thing off.
# reasoning-pro, NOT plain sonar — the opposite of the beat scout above, and
# measured: 16/18 careers pages survived the filter against sonar's 11 and
# sonar-pro's 11, naming Vercel, Linear, Supabase and Grafana Labs where sonar
# repeated six small names. sonar-pro twice returned a description instead of a
# company name. Re-measure with `pnpm sonar:check` before changing it.
AVA_MODEL_JOB_SCOUT=openrouter/perplexity/sonar-reasoning-pro

# ─── Leads (prospecting) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADR-0038. The other half of Joob: companies that should be hiring *you*,
# rather than roles you'd apply for. Delivered into the 🎯 Leads tab of the same
# chat. Nothing here runs until you've set a /client profile.
#
# Parses `/client` into a client_profiles row. Same shape as the job targeter,
# NOT the same model — measured: two live parses of one sentence took 9.0s and
# 232.8s on deepseek-v4-flash, the same spread that moved the translator off it.
# You are waiting on this one, and a four-minute parse reads as a broken bot.
AVA_MODEL_LEAD_TARGETER=openrouter/openai/gpt-5.6-luna
# Scores a batch of companies against your portfolio, on text alone. One call
# for the whole batch, portfolio in the prompt once. This is the CHEAP stage,
# and it exists so the expensive one below only ever sees leads worth the money.
AVA_MODEL_LEAD_SCORER=openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731
# The `sonar` lead source: which companies recently raised, launched or rebranded
# such that they now need what you sell. The third model here that needs the open
# web, and the same rule applies — it must actually search, or it names companies
# that raised in 2023 and the failure is silent.
# reasoning-pro, following the job scout's measurement rather than re-deriving it.
# Nothing it says is stored unverified: code fetches the company's own site,
# requires real text AND the company's name on the page, and fetches the
# announcement it cites. A prospect that resolves nothing is dropped — "does this
# company need better visuals" is checkable against nothing, so the parts that
# ARE checkable have to carry all the weight.
# Cost is capped by leadScout.maxProfilesPerRun in src/config/leads.ts — its own
# budget, deliberately NOT the job scout's. Before it existed, 37 of 39 leads came
# from postings the job scout happened to fetch, so prospecting reach was set by a
# number chosen for a different pipeline.
AVA_MODEL_LEAD_SCOUT=openrouter/perplexity/sonar-reasoning-pro
# Looks at a company's own images and says whether you could improve them.
# THE MOST EXPENSIVE CALL IN THIS REPO, and the only one that reads images.
# It exists because for visual work — photography, ad creative, design — the
# qualifying evidence *is* what their pictures look like, and every other stage
# here is structurally blind to that (mustHave scans text, htmlToText discards
# images, minPageChars is a text floor).
# Three bounds, all load-bearing, all in src/config/leads.ts rather than here:
#   1. it runs only on leads that already cleared the text score, one call each;
#   2. leadPipeline.maxVisionCallsPerRun is a hard stop per run;
#   3. the verdict is cached on the lead row for leadPipeline.visionStaleDays.
# Watch lead_runs.vision_calls — a run that hits the cap is telling you a filter
# or a threshold is letting too much through, before the invoice does.
AVA_MODEL_LEAD_VISION=openrouter/minimax/minimax-m3
# Writes the brief and the opening line for each lead that cleared the bar. A
# handful of calls a run, and the only lead-side output a human reads — so it is
# tuned like the summarizer rather than like the scorer.
AVA_MODEL_LEAD_WRITER=openrouter/openai/gpt-5.6-luna

# ─── Data ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mastra memory/workflows/scores + the app's own tables (feeds, items, feedback).
# Local dev: a file. Production: a Turso URL (libsql://…) keeps the container
# stateless, so no volume is needed and losing the box loses nothing.
AVA_DATA_DB_URL=file:./.data/ava.db
# Only needed for remote libsql/Turso URLs.
AVA_DATA_DB_AUTH_TOKEN=
# Where the Drizzle migrations live. A relative path anchors to the project root,
# NOT to the working directory — `mastra dev` runs the bundle from .mastra/.build
# and `mastra build` from .mastra/output, so a cwd-relative path fails with
# "Can't find meta/_journal.json". Absolute paths pass through untouched, which
# is what Docker sets. Leave this alone unless you are changing the image layout.
AVA_MIGRATIONS_DIR=drizzle

# ─── Observability ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Traces are read in Studio while tuning, so a local file is the right home.
AVA_TRACES_DB_URL=file:./.data/traces.db

# ─── Dev server ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Port Mastra listens on. Both surfaces read it: `pnpm dev` (Studio) and the
# built server behind `pnpm start`. Leave it empty and Mastra takes the first
# free port from 4111 upward — set it when 4111 is already taken, or when a
# second server has to sit beside the first.
# Terminal scripts follow it rather than assuming 4111: `pnpm workflow:run`
# builds its URL from src/config/dev-server.ts, which derives it from this.
# Docker is deliberately not wired to it — `EXPOSE 4111` and compose's
# `127.0.0.1:4111:4111` describe the container's layout, not this machine's.
PORT=
# Only for a server this machine cannot reach at localhost:PORT — another host,
# or a container being driven from outside. Overrides the whole URL, port
# included, so it wins over PORT above.
AVA_DEV_SERVER_URL=

# ─── Scheduling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IANA zone for digest fire times. Set explicitly so they don't follow server locale.
AGENT_TIMEZONE=Europe/Paris

# ─── Portfolio (Joob's CV source) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The CV Joob scores every job posting against. Fetched fresh at the start of
# each run, cached nowhere and distilled into nothing — a stale CV costs nothing
# because the next run is two days away, and a failed fetch costs nothing either
# because unscored postings simply wait for the next one. Update your portfolio
# and the next run uses it: no command, no redeploy. (ADR-0024)
#
# Must return markdown — `text/markdown` or `text/plain`. Anything else is
# rejected loudly, because a deployment-protection wall answers 200 with an HTML
# login page and that would otherwise be scored as if it were your CV.
# `pnpm portfolio:check` fetches it and shows you exactly what came back.
PORTFOLIO_FEED_URL=
# Optional. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`. Needed when the endpoint carries
# more than your public site does, which is the usual reason to have one.
PORTFOLIO_FEED_TOKEN=

# ─── Telegram ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One bot per agent, each with its own token — see ADR-0023. Only Telegram's
# `setMyCommands` accepts a scope; the name and both descriptions do not, so two
# agents behind one token would share one name and one profile page.
#
# Every bot's variables carry its name, including the first. Newy's were bare
# `TELEGRAM_*` while she was the only bot and were renamed on 2026-08-03: a bare
# name reads as "the default bot" once an explicit JOOB_ sits next to it.
#
# NOTE ON CHAT IDs: in a private chat Telegram's chat_id IS your user id, and
# user ids are global rather than per-bot — so both bots below take the SAME
# value here, and it is the same number as your entry in the allowlists. They
# are still two separate conversations in the client, because the token differs.
# (`telegram_topics` is keyed by bot id as well for exactly this reason.) Only
# the supergroup route gives the two bots different chat ids.

# --- Newy (news) ---
# From @BotFather.
NEWY_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated allowlist. Without this, anyone who finds the bot can use it.
NEWY_TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USER_IDS=
# Chat that scheduled digests are pushed to (usually your own user ID). Separate
# from the allowlist on purpose: that one says who may talk, this says where a
# cron fire — which has no message to reply to — should send.
NEWY_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# --- Joob (jobs) ---
# All optional. With no token Joob is constructed with no channel at all: it
# stays visible in Studio, polls nothing, publishes nothing, and costs nothing.
# A second @BotFather bot — NOT the same token as Newy's. Two adapters sharing a
# token share a getUpdates offset and eat each other's messages (invariant 7).
JOOB_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Required whenever the token is set: the adapter treats an empty allowlist as
# "everyone". Same person as Newy's, so usually the same IDs.
JOOB_TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USER_IDS=
# Same value as NEWY_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID on a private chat — see the note above.
JOOB_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# Threaded Mode is per bot and is a manual @BotFather toggle no API can set. A
# new bot needs its own: Bot Settings → Threads Settings → Threaded Mode.
# `pnpm telegram:check` reports it for both.
